+<h2 id="wi-fi-and-wireless">Wi-Fi and wireless</h2>
+<p>Wireless is not included in Gatehouse. The installer has no Wi-Fi drivers or firmware, the console has no Wi-Fi page, and a Wi-Fi card in the machine is ignored and cannot be used as an internet connection or a network. For Wi-Fi, plug a separate access point or Wi-Fi router into a port or VLAN on Networks, choose the type that fits (Guest for visitors), and set up the Wi-Fi name and password on that device. Gatehouse still filters, names and protects every device that connects through it, because they arrive over a wired port.</p>
 <h2 id="common-tasks">Common tasks</h2>
 <ol>
 <li>Add a network: <strong>Add a network</strong>, choose what it is for (<strong>Trusted</strong>, <strong>Private</strong>, <strong>Guest</strong>, <strong>Isolated</strong> or <strong>Custom</strong>), choose <strong>The whole port</strong> or <strong>A VLAN on the port</strong>, <strong>Save</strong>, <strong>Review and apply</strong>. A Guest network keeps its devices from seeing each other; turn on <strong>Devices here can talk to each other</strong> only if they need to.</li>
-<li>Connect a switch or access point that hands out addresses itself: choose <strong>A VLAN on the port</strong> and turn on <strong>No address, no DHCP</strong>. Gatehouse makes the VLAN and the firewall network and gives out nothing.</li>
+<li>Connect a switch or access point that hands out addresses itself (this is also how Wi-Fi is added; see Wi-Fi and wireless below): choose <strong>A VLAN on the port</strong> and turn on <strong>No address, no DHCP</strong>. Gatehouse makes the VLAN and the firewall network and gives out nothing.</li>
 <li>Name a port: <strong>Rename</strong>, type a name, <strong>Save</strong>. <strong>Use the original name</strong> puts it back.</li>
 <li>Give a network more ports: <strong>Edit</strong>, <strong>Add more ports to this network</strong>, tick them, <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
 </ol>
