-[{"file":"getting-started","chapter":"Getting started","heading":"Getting started","slug":"getting-started","text":"This guide takes you from a blank machine to a working network in about ten minutes. Everything you set up here can be changed later."},{"file":"getting-started","chapter":"Getting started","heading":"What you need","slug":"what-you-need","text":"A small computer with at least two wired network ports. It becomes your firewall, DNS server and DHCP server. The Gatehouse installer image, written to a USB stick. Your modem or provider's cable, and a switch or a computer for your own network."},{"file":"getting-started","chapter":"Getting started","heading":"Install","slug":"install","text":"Plug the USB stick into the machine and start it from the stick. Follow the installer. It asks which port is your internet and which is your own network. Plug in the cables as it says. When it finishes, remove the stick and restart."},{"file":"getting-started","chapter":"Getting started","heading":"First sign-in","slug":"first-sign-in","text":"On a computer plugged into your own network, open the address shown on the machine's screen. It looks like https://192.168.1.1. Your browser warns that the certificate is not trusted, because the box made its own. Continue anyway; the connection is still encrypted. Create the administrator account. Use at least 12 characters. A few random words is easy to remember and hard to guess. Answer How wil"},{"file":"getting-started","chapter":"Getting started","heading":"After setup","slug":"after-setup","text":"Open Overview. A short checklist shows what is left, such as turning on two-factor sign-in and making a first backup. Open Devices & access to name your devices and give them profiles. Make a backup under Settings, Backup & recovery."},{"file":"getting-started","chapter":"Getting started","heading":"If you cannot get in","slug":"if-you-cannot-get-in","text":"See [Getting back in](recovery.html). You will not be locked out for good: the machine's own screen always has a recovery menu."},{"file":"recovery","chapter":"If you are locked out","heading":"Getting back in","slug":"getting-back-in","text":"Gatehouse is built so that a wrong setting cannot lock you out for good. This page lists the ways back, from the easiest."},{"file":"recovery","chapter":"If you are locked out","heading":"A change went wrong and you can still see the console","slug":"a-change-went-wrong-and-you-can-still-see-the-console","text":"Every network and firewall change you apply starts a 60-second countdown. If you can still reach the console, the change is kept on its own. If you cannot, everything goes back by itself after the minute. To go back sooner, press Undo now on the countdown. To go back to an earlier point later, open Settings, Backup & recovery and use Changes. It keeps a copy from before each change. Pick a point t"},{"file":"recovery","chapter":"If you are locked out","heading":"You cannot reach the console from your computer","slug":"you-cannot-reach-the-console-from-your-computer","text":"Check the cable and that your computer is on the network you manage Gatehouse from. Look at the screen of the Gatehouse machine. It always shows the console address. Open that address in your browser. If you changed the console port and forgot it, the screen shows the port too."},{"file":"recovery","chapter":"If you are locked out","heading":"You are locked out of the console","slug":"you-are-locked-out-of-the-console","text":"Use the recovery screen on the machine's own keyboard and screen. It does not need the network, and it only works at the machine, never over the network. Press Alt+F2 on the machine to open the recovery screen. Choose what you need: set a new password for an account, clear an account's two-step sign-in, restore the safe starting firewall rules, put the console back on port 443, or remove any limit"},{"file":"recovery","chapter":"If you are locked out","heading":"You forgot your password, or lost your authenticator","slug":"you-forgot-your-password-or-lost-your-authenticator","text":"Both are fixed from the recovery screen above: set a new password, or clear two-step sign-in for the account and turn it on again after you are in."},{"file":"recovery","chapter":"If you are locked out","heading":"The internet is down","slug":"the-internet-is-down","text":"Open Overview or Internet. Each connection shows what is wrong in words: No link means check the cable or modem, No internet means the modem is answering but the provider is not. Restart the modem first. If you have two connections, Gatehouse uses the other one on its own."},{"file":"recovery","chapter":"If you are locked out","heading":"Last resort","slug":"last-resort","text":"Under Settings, Backup & recovery, Reset network settings puts networks, firewall rules, port forwards, DHCP, DNS names and lists, VPNs and Wi-Fi back to a clean start, and keeps your accounts, sign-in settings, update settings and console port. Factory reset erases everything and opens the setup wizard again. Both save a copy of your settings first, which you can download, and ask for your authen"},{"file":"overview","chapter":"Overview","heading":"Overview","slug":"overview","text":""},{"file":"overview","chapter":"Overview","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"One screen that tells you whether everything is fine, and what needs you. Open it first when something feels wrong.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"overview","chapter":"Overview","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"A status line under the title: a coloured dot, how many things need you, the first one as a link, and when the page last updated. The dot is red or amber when something needs you and green when nothing does. A box that lists the things that need you or are worth doing, such as a first backup or turning on two-factor sign-in, each with an Open button. The same things show as a small dot beside the "},{"file":"overview","chapter":"Overview","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"To see why the internet is down, look at the first card. It says in words what is wrong and links to Internet. To find the busiest device, read Busiest devices and click it. To follow a suggestion, press Open beside it. To start the map's lines from your home, open Settings, General, Your place on the map."},{"file":"overview","chapter":"Overview","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"The What happened card shows a device contacting a known-bad address nine times. Click the row to open Protection, or Open logs to see exactly which destinations they were."},{"file":"overview","chapter":"Overview","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"If a card is empty, it has nothing to show yet, or the machine has no sensor for it. If every card says it cannot refresh, check that you can still reach the machine, then press Try again now."},{"file":"internet","chapter":"Internet","heading":"Internet","slug":"internet","text":""},{"file":"internet","chapter":"Internet","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Where your internet comes from: the cable from your modem, how your provider connects you, and what to do when it stops.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"internet","chapter":"Internet","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Tabs along the top: Connections, Speed, Troubleshoot and Usage. Warnings stay above the tabs, and the tab you pick is remembered. Speed appears once a connection is switched on; the last two are for administrators. Connections: Internet connections: one row per connection with its state in words (Working, No link, No internet), its port and its delay. Add connection adds a second one. Edit a conne"},{"file":"internet","chapter":"Internet","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Add a second connection: Add connection, choose the port (Gatehouse notices a cable as you plug it in), choose how your provider connects you, Save, then Review and apply. Change provider settings: Edit, change the type, Save, Review and apply. Use two connections: give each an Order (1 is used first) or a Share of traffic."},{"file":"internet","chapter":"Internet","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Your cable modem needs nothing: choose Automatic. A fibre provider that needs a user name uses Username and password."},{"file":"internet","chapter":"Internet","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"No link means nothing answers on the cable: check the cable and the modem's power. No internet means the next device answers but nothing beyond it does: check your provider or restart the modem. A second connection takes over on its own."},{"file":"networks","chapter":"Networks","heading":"Networks","slug":"networks","text":""},{"file":"networks","chapter":"Networks","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Your own networks, such as Home, Guests or Cameras. Each one is separate; what it can reach is decided on the Firewall page.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"networks","chapter":"Networks","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Your networks: one row per network with its address, its ports and whether it hands out addresses (DHCP). Add a network makes another. Edit a network to change its Address of this router on the network, the Port it is plugged into, a VLAN number if it shares a port, and What it can reach. In use switches it off without deleting it. Ports on this network and Add more ports to this network: tick the"},{"file":"networks","chapter":"Networks","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Add a network: Add a network, choose what it is for (Trusted, Private, Guest, Isolated or Custom), choose The whole port or A VLAN on the port, Save, Review and apply. A Guest network keeps its devices from seeing each other; turn on Devices here can talk to each other only if they need to. Connect a switch or access point that hands out addresses itself: choose A VLAN on the port and turn on No a"},{"file":"networks","chapter":"Networks","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Make a Cameras network on its own port with the address 192.168.30.1/24, and on the Firewall page let it reach nothing but itself."},{"file":"networks","chapter":"Networks","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"Gatehouse refuses a port that already belongs to another network or the internet, and says which. Nothing changes until you apply, and the 60-second way back covers it."},{"file":"wifi","chapter":"Wi-Fi","heading":"Wi-Fi","slug":"wi-fi","text":""},{"file":"wifi","chapter":"Wi-Fi","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Use a Wi-Fi card in the machine to broadcast a network, or to join another network such as a hotspot.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"wifi","chapter":"Wi-Fi","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"One card per Wi-Fi adapter. What should this card do? offers Broadcast a Wi-Fi network or Join a Wi-Fi network. To broadcast: Network name, Password, Speed band (5 GHz is faster, 2.4 GHz reaches farther), Channel, Country this device is in, and Who will use this Wi-Fi? (My own devices, Same network as wired, Guests or Smart-home gadgets). Allow WPA3 and Hide the network name are options. To join: "},{"file":"wifi","chapter":"Wi-Fi","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Make a guest Wi-Fi: choose Broadcast a Wi-Fi network, name it, set a password, choose Guests, Save, Review and apply. Share the password: use the printable join card, which shows a QR code. Join a hotspot as your internet: choose Join a Wi-Fi network and pick it."},{"file":"wifi","chapter":"Wi-Fi","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Broadcast \"Home\" on 5 GHz for My own devices, and \"Home guests\" on 2.4 GHz for Guests."},{"file":"wifi","chapter":"Wi-Fi","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"This card cannot broadcast here means the card or the country setting does not allow it. Check Country this device is in. If the card stops answering, turn on Turn off power saving."},{"file":"dns","chapter":"DNS","heading":"DNS","slug":"dns","text":""},{"file":"dns","chapter":"DNS","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"DNS turns names into addresses. Gatehouse answers for your devices, blocks ads, trackers and malware names, and lets you add your own names.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"dns","chapter":"DNS","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Tabs along the top: Overview: Lookups over time, How queries were answered, Busiest devices, Most blocked and Most asked. Blocking: Blocklists you can switch on, Always allow or block exceptions that beat every list, and Why is this blocked? to find which list blocks a name. Servers: Upstream DNS servers (where Gatehouse asks when it does not know), Which server is asked first, Add a DNS server, a"},{"file":"dns","chapter":"DNS","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Allow a blocked site: Always allow or block, type the name, choose allow, Add. Find out why a site is blocked: Why is this blocked?, type the name, Check. Name a device on your network: Your own names, enter the name and address, Add. Block an app, a topic or a website for one device, a network or at set hours: this is on the Protection page now, under Apps & topics. See what a list blocks: Blocki"},{"file":"dns","chapter":"DNS","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"A shopping site does not load. Type its name under Why is this blocked?; it names the list. Add the name to Always allow or block as allowed."},{"file":"dns","chapter":"DNS","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"No server is answering under Servers means none of the upstream servers replied. Check the internet. Clear saved answers forgets everything Gatehouse remembers and is safe to use."},{"file":"dhcp","chapter":"DHCP","heading":"DHCP","slug":"dhcp","text":""},{"file":"dhcp","chapter":"DHCP","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"DHCP gives every device on a network an address when it joins. Gatehouse does this for you.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"dhcp","chapter":"DHCP","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Address pools: one per network, with First address to give out, Last address to give out, Gateway, DNS server, How long an address is kept and Home domain. Who hands out addresses here? is Gatehouse, Another router, relayed to Gatehouse, or Another device (Gatehouse stays out of it). Check for other servers listens for a second DHCP server, which causes trouble. Devices with an address: every devi"},{"file":"dhcp","chapter":"DHCP","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Keep a printer at the same address: find it under Devices with an address, press Reserve. Change the range: edit the pool and change the first and last address. Make a pool for a new network: add the network on the Networks page first; it appears here."},{"file":"dhcp","chapter":"DHCP","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Reserve 192.168.1.20 for the printer, then use that address in a firewall rule or port forward."},{"file":"dhcp","chapter":"DHCP","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"If devices get addresses you did not expect, Check for other servers can show another router handing out addresses. Turn that one's DHCP off."},{"file":"firewall","chapter":"Firewall","heading":"Firewall","slug":"firewall","text":""},{"file":"firewall","chapter":"Firewall","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Decides what each of your networks may reach, and lets you add exceptions. The type of each network does most of the work in plain terms.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"firewall","chapter":"Firewall","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Tabs along the top: Networks: Your networks, each with a type. For each: Can reach the internet, Devices here can talk to each other, Can manage Gatehouse, and under Advanced Gatehouse is the gateway, Make devices here use the enforced DNS server and Allow remote login (SSH). Test my access shows whether a device can reach something. In plain words says what the firewall does right now. Rules: you"},{"file":"firewall","chapter":"Firewall","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Open a port for a game console: Open ports, Open a port, choose the app, choose the device, Save, Review and apply. Stop a network reaching the internet: Networks, turn off Can reach the internet for it. Allow one device to reach another network: Rules, add a rule, choose the device and the service. Open a port in three taps: Open ports, Open a port, tap the app, tap the device. Gatehouse tells yo"},{"file":"firewall","chapter":"Firewall","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Open Plex for your media server: Open a port, choose Plex, choose the server, optionally limit it to your own country, Save, Review and apply."},{"file":"firewall","chapter":"Firewall","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"Gatehouse refuses a change that would leave you no way to manage it, and always keeps safe starting rules above everything you can edit. If a change breaks something, the 60-second way back puts it right."},{"file":"protection","chapter":"Protection","heading":"Protection","slug":"protection","text":""},{"file":"protection","chapter":"Protection","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"One place for keeping the network safe: a check of what to fix, blocking apps and topics, and watching for threats. Block lists for ads, trackers and adult sites stay under DNS."},{"file":"protection","chapter":"Protection","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Tabs along the top: Overview: Protection at a glance gives one sentence of verdict and a line each for the security check, your blocks and threat watching. Below it, the Security check lists what to fix, with a button that goes to the right page. Apps & topics: Block apps, topics and websites. Add a block chooses what to block (an app, a topic such as adult, gambling or games, or a website), for w"},{"file":"protection","chapter":"Protection","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"See what needs fixing: Overview, read the Security check, press the button beside a risk. Block an app for a child's device: Apps & topics, Add a block, choose the app, choose the device, choose the hours, Save. Turn on threat watching: Threats, Install threat watching, read the sizes, Install. When it finishes, choose Watch and tell. Quiet a harmless alert: Alerts, Allow for this device. Lower it"},{"file":"protection","chapter":"Protection","heading":"What happens if something goes wrong","slug":"what-happens-if-something-goes-wrong","text":"These features only add protection. If they fail, the protection goes and your internet carries on: your traffic is never held up. A block that cannot be worked out lets the lookup through, and the Logs say so once an hour. Threat watching reads copies of the traffic. If it stops, crashes or is removed, nothing changes for your devices. If it uses all of its processor share for ten minutes, Gateho"},{"file":"protection","chapter":"Protection","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"A new camera starts talking to an address on a bad list. The Threats tab shows \"A device may be infected and talking to a known bad server\", with the camera's name. You unplug it, or choose Never check this device if you know it is fine."},{"file":"protection","chapter":"Protection","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"If the page says The program keeps failing, the program starts and stops again and again; read the reason, then Program log under Advanced (or Diagnostics in the left menu). If Install threat watching fails, the page shows the reason; try again once the internet works and there is disk space. If the page says the program is not running, your traffic is not affected and Gatehouse keeps trying to st"},{"file":"services","chapter":"Services","heading":"Services","slug":"services","text":""},{"file":"services","chapter":"Services","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"A service is a name for one or more ports, such as \"plex\" for tcp/32400. Rules pick services by name instead of by number.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"services","chapter":"Services","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Search services: type a name or a port. Your services: the ones you made, with New service, Edit and Delete. Deleting one can be undone for a few seconds, unless a rule uses it. Built in: names Gatehouse already knows. They cannot be changed."},{"file":"services","chapter":"Services","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Make a service: New service, give it a Name, enter Ports, one per line (for example tcp/32400 or udp/27000-27050), Save. Use it: in any rule or open port, type its name in the \"What for\" box. Several can go in one box, separated by commas or spaces. Find a built-in one: type its name in Search services. Rename a service you made: Edit, change the Name, Save. Rules and open ports that use it follow"},{"file":"services","chapter":"Services","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Make \"console-game\" with udp/3074 and tcp/3074, then use it in a rule for your console."},{"file":"services","chapter":"Services","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"A service that a rule uses cannot be deleted until the rule changes; Gatehouse names the rules."},{"file":"vpn","chapter":"VPN","heading":"VPN","slug":"vpn","text":""},{"file":"vpn","chapter":"VPN","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Send some devices through a VPN provider, let your phone reach home from anywhere, or join two sites together.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"vpn","chapter":"VPN","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Add a VPN starts a guided choice: use a provider's file, let devices reach home, or Join two sites. Each VPN has a card with its state in words. For a provider VPN: Who uses it? (Whole networks, Device groups or Individual devices), Kill switch, Test address, Internet sees you as and Checked. Check beside a device shows, by itself every few seconds, whether its connections and name lookups really "},{"file":"vpn","chapter":"VPN","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Send your computer through a provider: Add a VPN, drop the provider's file, choose Individual devices and pick the computer, Add and connect. Reach home from your phone: Add a VPN, choose to reach home, name the first device, scan the QR code. Check it works: press Check next to the device."},{"file":"vpn","chapter":"VPN","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Set Kill switch on for a device that must never use your normal connection. If the VPN drops, that device loses internet rather than leaking."},{"file":"vpn","chapter":"VPN","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"Needs attention says what failed. If a device shows your normal connection under Internet sees you as, press Check, then Review and apply if it says the address changed. With the kill switch off, name lookups fall back to the normal connection while the VPN is down, and the card says so."},{"file":"routes","chapter":"Routes","heading":"Routes","slug":"routes","text":""},{"file":"routes","chapter":"Routes","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Send some traffic through a VPN by rule: everything from certain devices, or only some destinations.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"routes","chapter":"Routes","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Your routes: the first route that matches is used, so put the more specific ones at the top with Move up and Move down. Add a route takes a Name, Send the traffic through a VPN, Kill switch, Who uses this route? and Where does it apply? (Everything or Only some destinations, by Website, Country or Address or range). VPNs that send whole devices through them are set up on the VPN page and shown bel"},{"file":"routes","chapter":"Routes","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Send one website through a VPN: Add, choose Website, type it, choose the VPN, Save. Send a country's traffic: choose Country. Pause a route without deleting it: Pause. Remove deletes it and can be undone for a few seconds."},{"file":"routes","chapter":"Routes","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"A streaming app only works from another country: add a route for its website through a VPN in that country, for your TV only."},{"file":"routes","chapter":"Routes","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"Not working means the VPN behind the route is down. Needs two connections means a route needs more than one internet connection. With Kill switch on, the traffic stops rather than leaking."},{"file":"profiles","chapter":"Profiles","heading":"Profiles","slug":"profiles","text":""},{"file":"profiles","chapter":"Profiles","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"A profile decides which blocklists apply to a device and when the internet is off for it, such as a Kids profile.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"profiles","chapter":"Profiles","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Profiles: each with a Name, How much to filter, Safe search, and Internet off at set hours (Off from and Back on at). Pause buttons stop the internet for that profile's devices for a while, and Resume internet undoes it. Add a profile: start from Blank profile, Basic or Enhanced, then change anything. Filtering per network: choose a profile for a whole network. Devices that have a profile of their"},{"file":"profiles","chapter":"Profiles","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Pause a profile: press the pause length you want on the profile. Make a Kids profile: Add profile, choose a preset, name it, set the hours, Save. Put a device in a profile: on Devices & access, open the device and choose its Profile."},{"file":"profiles","chapter":"Profiles","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Kids: Enhanced filtering, Safe search on, internet off from 21:00 to 07:00."},{"file":"profiles","chapter":"Profiles","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"Deleting a profile moves its devices back to the Default profile. Safe search works for browsers that use Gatehouse for DNS; turn on forced DNS for devices that do not."},{"file":"devices","chapter":"Devices & access","heading":"Devices & access","slug":"devices-access","text":""},{"file":"devices","chapter":"Devices & access","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Every device that has joined your network. Rename it, give it an icon, put it in a profile, or pause its internet.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"devices","chapter":"Devices & access","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Search devices and a List or Map view. Columns and rows chooses what the table shows. Each row shows the device, its network, how it is connected and whether it is allowed online. A button on the row pauses or resumes it; an offline device has Forget. Open a device to change its Name, Icon, Profile, DNS filtering, Notes and Fixed address, or Pause the internet for a set time. Allowed online turns "},{"file":"devices","chapter":"Devices & access","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Pause a device: press its row button, or open it and choose a pause length. Resume now brings it back. Rename a device: click it, type the name, press Enter. Keep a device at the same address: open it, turn on Fixed address. Let the internet reach an app on a device: press Open a port on its row, tap the app, done. Then Review and apply at the bottom."},{"file":"devices","chapter":"Devices & access","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Pause the games console for two hours at homework time: open it and choose the two-hour pause. It comes back by itself."},{"file":"devices","chapter":"Devices & access","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"A device missing from the list has not joined yet, or has not asked Gatehouse for an address. Use Add a device to record it. If a name is wrong, rename it; the old name is only a guess from the network."},{"file":"logs","chapter":"Logs","heading":"Logs","slug":"logs","text":""},{"file":"logs","chapter":"Logs","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"What happened on your network and why: connections the firewall allowed or blocked, names your devices looked up, and changes.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"logs","chapter":"Logs","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Tabs: Log, Summary & map and Activity. The search box and filters: Allowed and blocked, a time range (1 h, 24 h, 7 d, 30 d, or custom) and Everything, Connections, Names (DNS) or Events. Live updates can be turned off. Every row is at most two lines: the app or website on top, the port and service below. Out through shows which connection or VPN it left by. Identical neighbouring entries become on"},{"file":"logs","chapter":"Logs","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Find out why something was blocked: search for the device or site, click the row, read Why. Block a destination: click its row, then Block this. See which devices use a VPN: look at Out through. Each row shows the internet connection or VPN it left through. For a DNS lookup that is the connection Gatehouse itself used to ask the DNS server; a lookup answered from memory shows none. Protection feat"},{"file":"logs","chapter":"Logs","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Search for a game console, set the range to 1 h, and click a blocked row. Allow this makes a rule for exactly that traffic."},{"file":"logs","chapter":"Logs","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"If nothing is recorded, check Recording & storage; allowed and blocked connections can be switched off separately. Only the first packet of each connection is recorded, and a flood is capped."},{"file":"diagnostics","chapter":"Diagnostics","heading":"Diagnostics","slug":"diagnostics","text":""},{"file":"diagnostics","chapter":"Diagnostics","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Shows how each part of Gatehouse is doing, lets you read its log, test the connection, and, if you switch it on, open a command line on the firewall. It is for the day something is not working and there is no screen or keyboard on the machine."},{"file":"diagnostics","chapter":"Diagnostics","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Tabs along the top: Services: each part of the system with a state: Running, Starting, Failing or Off. A failing one says why in plain words, such as the last thing it complained about. Show log opens its log and Restart restarts it. Save a support bundle saves a text file with the machine, the network, every service's state and recent log lines. Anything that looks like a password or key is hidde"},{"file":"diagnostics","chapter":"Diagnostics","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Find out why a service is not working: Services, read the reason, press Show log. Restart a service that is stuck: Restart. Get help: Save a support bundle, and send the file. Run a command by hand: Terminal, Switch the terminal on, enter your password or code, then Open a terminal."},{"file":"diagnostics","chapter":"Diagnostics","heading":"The terminal","slug":"the-terminal","text":"A terminal is a full command line with the highest rights on the machine. Anyone who can sign in as an administrator and pass the check can do anything on it, so Gatehouse treats it carefully: It is off until you switch it on, and only administrators can use it. Switching it on asks for your password, or your authenticator code if you use one. Opening it asks again only if the last check was more "},{"file":"diagnostics","chapter":"Diagnostics","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"If a service shows Failing, read its reason and its log; the reason is the last line it printed that looked like an error. If the page itself shows nothing, you may not be signed in as an administrator. If the terminal will not open, check that it is switched on and that two terminals are not already open. If the terminal stops responding, Close the terminal and open a new one."},{"file":"settings","chapter":"Settings","heading":"Settings","slug":"settings","text":""},{"file":"settings","chapter":"Settings","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"Your account, and how this box is run. It is split into five tabs.  The picture is an example from a new, empty box; yours will show your own devices and numbers."},{"file":"settings","chapter":"Settings","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"General: Finish setting up (only while something is left) and Common setups, Device mode (Router or Bridge, with a three-step change), Updates (Check now, How often to check, Off, Notify or Automatic), Power (restart or shut down), Console address, Your place on the map (click the map or pick a country; it stays on this machine) and Appearance. Account & sign-in: Two-factor sign-in, Password, Wher"},{"file":"settings","chapter":"Settings","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Change mode: General, Device mode, Change, then follow the three steps. Nothing is saved until the last step, and you have 60 seconds to keep it. Turn on two-factor sign-in: Account & sign-in, Turn on, scan the code. Make a backup: Backup & recovery, Back up now."},{"file":"settings","chapter":"Settings","heading":"Example","slug":"example","text":"Use Notify for updates: Gatehouse tells you when one is ready and you press Update now when it suits you."},{"file":"settings","chapter":"Settings","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"A mode change that is not kept goes back on its own, and the card says so. See Getting back in for lock-outs."},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"Reference","slug":"reference","text":""},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"Ports Gatehouse uses","slug":"ports-gatehouse-uses","text":"443: the console (HTTPS). You can change it under Settings, General, Console address. 53: DNS, on your networks only. 67 and 68: DHCP, on the networks where it is switched on. 22: remote login (SSH). Off unless you turn on Remote login under Settings, Account & sign-in. It is never reachable from the internet side."},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"What Apply does","slug":"what-apply-does","text":"Changes to networks and the firewall are saved first and applied when you press Review and apply. Gatehouse checks the new firewall before loading it. After loading, a 60-second countdown starts. If your browser can still reach the console, the change is kept automatically. If not, it is undone. Undo now and Keep this change let you decide sooner."},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"The order things are checked in","slug":"the-order-things-are-checked-in","text":"Firewall: each network has a type. The type decides what it can reach. Rules you add are checked from the top; the first match wins. DNS: your own always-allow and always-block exceptions beat every list. Routes: the first route that matches is used, so put the more specific ones at the top. VPN routing: a device sent through a VPN is routed by its address. Its name lookups go through the VPN too."},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"When a protection feature fails","slug":"when-a-protection-feature-fails","text":"Blocking apps and topics, and threat watching, only add protection. If they fail, your internet carries on and the protection is lost; your traffic is never held up. A block that cannot be worked out lets the lookup through, and the Logs say so (once an hour). Threat watching reads copies of the traffic, so if it stops, crashes or is removed nothing changes for your devices; if it uses all of its "},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"Updates","slug":"updates","text":"Gatehouse looks for updates every 12 hours unless you change How often to check (6 hours, 12 hours, a day, a week, or your own number of hours from 1 to 720). Under Settings, General, Updates you choose Off (only when you press Check now), Notify (it tells you) or Automatic (it installs once a day in the hour you choose). Updates are signed; Gatehouse installs only releases signed with its own key"},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"Backups","slug":"backups","text":"Backups are made on a schedule under Settings, Backup & recovery. A backup is only worth having if it restores, so each one is checked as it is made. Keep a copy on another computer or drive."},{"file":"reference","chapter":"Reference","heading":"Words used in the console","slug":"words-used-in-the-console","text":"Network: a separate group of devices, such as Home or Guests. Each is one port, several ports, or a VLAN. Profile: which blocklists and hours apply to a device. Kill switch: if a VPN drops, the devices using it lose internet instead of falling back to the normal connection. Pair: two Gatehouse machines working together; if one fails, the other takes over."},{"file":"ease-of-use","chapter":"How easy is it?","heading":"How easy is it?","slug":"how-easy-is-it","text":"Gatehouse aims for no more than three steps for anything you do often. This page lists the everyday jobs, how many steps each takes today, and where the answer is still more than three. A step is one click, or one thing typed or chosen. Opening the page from the side menu counts as one. These counts come from walking through the screens, not from watching people use them."},{"file":"ease-of-use","chapter":"How easy is it?","heading":"What it is for","slug":"what-it-is-for","text":"It shows you what to expect before you start, and it is the list the next round of improvements is taken from."},{"file":"ease-of-use","chapter":"How easy is it?","heading":"What is on the page","slug":"what-is-on-the-page","text":"Everyday jobs and their steps Cut a device off the internet: Devices, then Pause on its row. 2 steps. To choose how long, open the row first. 3 steps. Pause everyone in a profile: Profiles, then a Pause choice. 2 steps. Find out why the internet is down: open Overview. The top of the page says Internet is down and gives the reason. 1 step. Rename a device: Devices, click the device, type the name,"},{"file":"ease-of-use","chapter":"How easy is it?","heading":"Common tasks","slug":"common-tasks","text":"Check a job before you start: find it in the list above. Report a job that feels long: say which one, so it can be added."},{"file":"ease-of-use","chapter":"How easy is it?","heading":"If something goes wrong","slug":"if-something-goes-wrong","text":"Nothing here changes any setting. The jobs that are over three steps and not yet shortened are renaming a device by mouse (4), adding a second connection (4), adding a VPN (4 or more) and opening a port (6). Opening a port by hand is the longest, and the first thing to shorten."}]
